//? Title: Blossom Matching: Contract an Odd Cycle, Lift an Augmenting Path
//? Subtitle: Actual contraction sets, original-vertex paths, maximum-size certificates and search work
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Select as many graph edges as possible while using each vertex at most once.
In a general graph, odd cycles make a straightforward alternating-tree search
insufficient. Edmonds' blossom method contracts a suitable odd cycle and lifts
the resulting route back to original vertices. We inspect a four-vertex case,
retain the original six-vertex example, and check graph families with elementary
optimality certificates. The native result and trace share one kernel, so the
reported contractions, lifted paths and logical work are actual execution records.
)

\toc

> The method and its correctness

A matching M has disjoint endpoints. An augmenting path starts and ends at free
vertices and alternates between edges outside and inside M. Flipping its edges
adds one pair. Berge's criterion says that M is maximum exactly when no such
path exists. To see the reverse direction, compare M with a larger matching N:
their symmetric difference consists of alternating paths and cycles, and some
path must contain one more N-edge than M-edge. That component augments M.

The search grows an alternating tree from a free root. An edge between suitable
even-level branches closes an odd cycle whose base is their common ancestor.
Contract this alternating-tree blossom into its base. Its internal matching
leaves just the base available to the stem; the cycle's two routes to another
vertex have opposite parity, allowing the contracted route to be lifted with
the required alternation. Repeated contraction preserves augmenting-path
existence for these rooted blossoms. The core represents contraction with base
labels and redirects parent links for lifting; it does not mutate the caller's
graph.

> Simple example: a route that uses the blossom

Take triangle 0–1–2–0 with an extra edge 1–3. The first augmentation matches
0–1. Searching from free vertex 2 initially reaches 1 at odd level and 0 at
even level. The closing triangle edge contracts members [0,1,2] to base 2,
allowing the exit at vertex 1 to be used. The lifted augmenting path is
2–0–1–3. Its first and last edges enter the matching; 0–1 leaves it.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/blossom_lift.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::vector<std::pair<std::size_t,std::size_t>> edges{{0,1},{1,2},{2,0},{1,3}};
  const auto trace=dm::algorithms::blossom_maximum_matching_trace(4,edges);
  const std::vector<dm::graphics::DiagramPoint> position{{-1,0},{1,0},{0,1.6},{2.8,-.8}};
  dm::graphics::Diagram diagram(75,1100);
  for (std::size_t panel=0;panel<2;++panel) {
    const double shift=6.3*panel;
    const auto& matching=trace.augmentations[panel].matching;
    for (const auto& [u,v]:edges) {
      dm::graphics::DiagramStroke stroke;stroke.color={203,213,225};
      if (matching.mate[u]==v) {stroke.color=panel?dm::RGB{22,163,74}:dm::RGB{37,99,235};stroke.width=4;}
      if (!panel) {
        const auto& path=trace.augmentations.back().path;
        for (std::size_t j=0;j+1<path.size();++j)
          if (((path[j]==u&&path[j+1]==v)||(path[j]==v&&path[j+1]==u)) && matching.mate[u]!=v) {
            stroke.color={220,38,38};stroke.width=3;stroke.dashed=true;
          }
      }
      diagram.path({{position[u].x+shift,position[u].y},{position[v].x+shift,position[v].y}},stroke);
    }
    if (!panel) {
      dm::graphics::DiagramStroke boundary;boundary.color={124,58,237};boundary.dashed=true;
      diagram.ellipse({shift,.55},1.65,1.65,dm::RGB{255,255,255,0},boundary);
    }
    dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle vertex;vertex.circle=true;vertex.width=.5;vertex.height=.5;vertex.padding=5;
    for (std::size_t v=0;v<position.size();++v)
      diagram.node(std::format("{}-{}",panel,v),{position[v].x+shift,position[v].y},std::to_string(v),vertex);
    diagram.text({shift+.8,2.8},panel?"After lifting: two matched pairs":"Before: one pair; blossom base 2");
  }
  diagram.text({3.8,-2.5},"Blue: old matching; red dashed: added path edges; green: augmented matching.");
  diagram.save_svg("build/blossom_lift.svg");
  std::print("| search | contracted base | original members |\n| ---: | ---: | :--- |\n");
  for (const auto& event:trace.contractions)
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} |\n",event.search_index,event.base,event.members);
  std::print("\n| search | lifted path | matching size after flip |\n| ---: | :--- | ---: |\n");
  for (const auto& event:trace.augmentations)
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} |\n",event.search_index,event.path,event.matching.size);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| search | contracted base | original members |
| ---: | ---: | :--- |
| 1 | 2 | [0, 1, 2] |

| search | lifted path | matching size after flip |
| ---: | :--- | ---: |
| 0 | [0, 1] | 1 |
| 1 | [2, 0, 1, 3] | 2 |
\image(figures/blossom_lift.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The first panel shows the old matching and the actual lifted path inside an outlined odd blossom. The second shows the two resulting pairs. Event members are sorted vertex sets, rather than a cyclic ordering.)
\alttext(A triangle on vertices zero, one and two has a tail from one to three. Initially zero is matched with one. The path two to zero to one to three replaces that pair by two to zero and one to three.)

The direct triangle route 2–1 followed by 1–3 would use two consecutive
unmatched edges. The even-length internal route 2–0–1 supplies the required
matched edge 0–1 before exiting. The trace stores this original-vertex path,
including the parent-link changes that implement lifting.

> Original six-vertex example and unmatched vertices

Retain the original triangle, stem 1–3–4, and edge 2–5. The returned three
pairs reach the universal bound \(\lfloor6/2\rfloor=3\), certifying
maximum cardinality. Adding an isolated seventh vertex keeps that bound and
matching size, while exposing the public unmatched sentinel.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const std::vector<std::pair<std::size_t,std::size_t>> edges{{0,1},{1,2},{2,0},{1,3},{3,4},{2,5}};
  const auto original=dm::algorithms::blossom_maximum_matching(6,edges);
  std::print("Original six-vertex matching size: {}. Pairs:",original.size);
  for (std::size_t v=0;v<original.mate.size();++v)
    if (original.mate[v]!=dm::algorithms::kGeneralMatchingUnmatched && v<original.mate[v])
      std::print(" {}-{}",v,original.mate[v]);
  std::print(".\n\n");
  const auto extended=dm::algorithms::blossom_maximum_matching(7,edges);
  std::print("| vertex with isolated-vertex control | partner |\n| ---: | :--- |\n");
  for (std::size_t v=0;v<extended.mate.size();++v)
    if (extended.mate[v]==dm::algorithms::kGeneralMatchingUnmatched) std::print("| {} | unmatched |\n",v);
    else std::print("| {} | {} |\n",v,extended.mate[v]);
  if (original.size!=3 || extended.size!=3) return 1;
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Original six-vertex matching size: 3. Pairs: 0-1 2-5 3-4.

| vertex with isolated-vertex control | partner |
| ---: | :--- |
| 0 | 1 |
| 1 | 0 |
| 2 | 5 |
| 3 | 4 |
| 4 | 3 |
| 5 | 2 |
| 6 | unmatched |
// result_end
\caption(The sentinel is checked before indexing or emitting an edge. Testing only v<mate[v] would also accept an unmatched vertex because the sentinel is SIZE_MAX.)

> Checking the theory: constructive upper bounds

Any matching uses two vertices per edge, so \(|M|\le\lfloor V/2\rfloor\).
Every matching edge must also use a different vertex of any vertex cover C,
giving \(|M|\le|C|\). Finally, disconnected components cannot share a
matching edge, so their separate bounds add.

These facts certify the generated cases below. A complete eight-vertex graph
has four disjoint pairs. A seven-cycle has three. An eight-vertex star has a
one-vertex cover at its centre and can match only one leaf. Four disconnected
triangles contribute at most one pair each, giving four rather than the loose
whole-graph bound of six. Each returned matching attains the stated bound.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::print("| graph family | vertices | edges | returned pairs | certified upper bound |\n"
             "| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (const auto& [family,n]:std::vector<std::pair<std::string,std::size_t>>{
       {"complete",8},{"cycle",7},{"star",8},{"four triangles",12}}) {
    std::vector<std::pair<std::size_t,std::size_t>> edges;
    if (family=="complete")
      for (std::size_t u=0;u<n;++u) for (std::size_t v=u+1;v<n;++v) edges.emplace_back(u,v);
    if (family=="cycle") for (std::size_t u=0;u<n;++u) edges.emplace_back(u,(u+1)%n);
    if (family=="star") for (std::size_t u=1;u<n;++u) edges.emplace_back(0,u);
    if (family=="four triangles") for (std::size_t u=0;u<n;u+=3)
      for (std::size_t j=0;j<3;++j) edges.emplace_back(u+j,u+(j+1)%3);
    const auto matching=dm::algorithms::blossom_maximum_matching(n,edges);
    const std::size_t upper=family=="star"?1:family=="four triangles"?4:n/2;
    if (matching.size!=upper) return 1;
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",family,n,edges.size(),matching.size,upper);
  }
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| graph family | vertices | edges | returned pairs | certified upper bound |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| complete | 8 | 28 | 4 | 4 |
| cycle | 7 | 7 | 3 | 3 |
| star | 8 | 7 | 1 | 1 |
| four triangles | 12 | 12 | 4 | 4 |
// result_end
\caption(Each upper bound has a separate elementary proof, so attaining it certifies these cases without relying on the matching algorithm's own stopping condition.)

Native tests additionally enumerate all 33,868 simple graphs on zero through
six vertices. An independent subset recurrence either leaves the first available
vertex free or pairs it with each available neighbour, obtaining the exact
optimum. Tests compare both core entry points with that result, validate every
lifted path and post-flip snapshot, and check internal pairing of each odd set.
One thousand shuffled random graphs through twelve vertices and a fixed nested
blossom control extend that finite check. The general correctness argument
remains the alternating-path and contraction theory.

> Checking work and accounting for duplicate edges

Each root search resets its arrays, queues each original vertex at most once,
and scans at most 2E adjacency entries. Contractions merge representatives;
there are O(V) contractions per search, each with O(V) ancestor, parent and
base work. At most V root searches therefore cost
\(O(V^3+VE)\), using \(O(V+E)\) unrecorded storage.
For a simple graph E=O(V²), this is the familiar cubic bound. Accepted duplicate
edges remain in adjacency order, so their extra scans must be included when E
counts the supplied edge list. Self-loops are validated, then ignored.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/blossom_scans.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::vector<double> complete_n,complete_scans,cycle_n,cycle_scans;
  double largest=1;
  std::print("| family | vertices | pairs | root searches | contractions | edge scans | scan bound 2E searches |\n"
             "| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (std::size_t size=4;size<=64;size+=4) for (bool cycle:{false,true}) {
    const std::size_t n=size+(cycle?1:0);
    std::vector<std::pair<std::size_t,std::size_t>> edges;
    if (cycle) for (std::size_t u=0;u<n;++u) edges.emplace_back(u,(u+1)%n);
    else for (std::size_t u=0;u<n;++u) for (std::size_t v=u+1;v<n;++v) edges.emplace_back(u,v);
    const auto trace=dm::algorithms::blossom_maximum_matching_trace(n,edges);
    const auto bound=2*edges.size()*trace.searches;
    if (trace.result.size!=n/2 || trace.edge_scans>bound) return 1;
    (cycle?cycle_n:complete_n).push_back(n);
    (cycle?cycle_scans:complete_scans).push_back(trace.edge_scans);
    largest=std::max(largest,double(trace.edge_scans));
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",cycle?"odd cycle":"complete",n,
        trace.result.size,trace.searches,trace.contractions.size(),trace.edge_scans,bound);
  }
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .lines(complete_n,complete_scans,"complete scans",dm::RGB{37,99,235},2)
      .lines(cycle_n,cycle_scans,"odd cycle scans",dm::RGB{217,119,6},2)
      .title("Actual adjacency scans in two generated graph families")
      .x_label("vertices V").y_label("inspected adjacency entries")
      .y_limits(0,1.5*largest).size(900,480).save_svg("build/blossom_scans.svg");
}
```
