\caption(The counter includes entries skipped as matched edges or already-contracted edges. It measures adjacency inspections, rather than wall time or all ancestor/base work.)
\alttext(Adjacency-scan counts grow with graph size, with many more scans in complete graphs than in odd cycles. The table also reports searches, contractions and the per-execution scan upper bound.)

Recording is deliberately more expensive in space: a contraction copies its
expanded original-vertex set, and an augmentation copies its path and mate map.
There can be O(V²) contractions, so the recorded trace may occupy O(V³+E)
storage. The ordinary function allocates no event records. Both use the same
search and return the same matching for the same adjacency order.

> Conclusion

The four-vertex example shows an actual odd-cycle contraction and a lifted
path whose flip increases the matching from one pair to two. The original
six-vertex example still returns three pairs. Explicit unmatched handling,
family-specific upper bounds and exhaustive small-graph comparisons make
the outputs checkable. Native traces replace any report-side reconstruction
of contraction and lifting.

The API now rejects vertex counts beyond INT_MAX before allocating graph
storage and validates every endpoint. It computes unweighted maximum cardinality;
different tied optima can depend on edge order. Generated graphs are sufficient
for these structural properties, so no observed dataset is needed.

> References

- Berge, C. (1957). *Two theorems in graph theory*. Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences, 43(9), 842–844. https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.43.9.842.
- Edmonds, J. (1965). *Paths, trees, and flowers*. Canadian Journal of Mathematics, 17, 449–467. https://doi.org/10.4153/CJM-1965-045-4. The implementation uses an array-based unweighted search with parent-link lifting.
